  <h1>How Lemon Law Works</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Qualifying thresholds</h2><p>Most states require one of: (a) the same defect persists after 3–4 repair attempts; (b) a safety defect persists after 1–2 repair attempts; or (c) the vehicle has been out of service for 30+ cumulative days for warranty repairs. California, New York, and Texas are among the strongest lemon law states.</p>
  <h2>The repurchase formula</h2><p>Refund = (Purchase price + taxes + registration + finance charges) − Mileage offset. Mileage offset = (Miles before first defect report / 120,000 [or state-specific divisor]) × purchase price. Incidental damages (rental car costs, towing) are separately recoverable in many states.</p>
  <h2>Mandatory attorney fees</h2><p>This is the critical provision: virtually all state lemon laws require the manufacturer to pay the consumer&rsquo;s attorney fees if the consumer prevails. This means consumers can hire specialized lemon law attorneys with no out-of-pocket cost. Manufacturers know this, which is why most lemon law claims settle before trial.</p>
  <h2>Dispute resolution</h2><p>Manufacturers must provide access to an approved arbitration program before the consumer can sue in some states. California&rsquo;s AUTOCAP and BBB Auto Line are common programs. Arbitration is optional for the consumer (not mandatory) in California; other states vary. If arbitration fails, you proceed to court.</p>
